WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:08.423
PMM: Thanks for making time. This is a deal review, not a forecast call, so I'm not grading anything.

2
00:00:08.423 --> 00:00:15.346
PMM: I just want what happened. Start at the beginning. What happened at the customer that started this evaluation?

3
00:00:16.146 --> 00:00:22.813
Dana: So, honestly, I thought it was the online ordering portal. That's what the inbound said.

4
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:29.479
Dana: They sell to restaurants, kitchen equipment, disposables, all of that, and all their ordering was reps and phone calls.

5
00:00:29.479 --> 00:00:36.146
Dana: They were building a portal so a chef could reorder at midnight. So I went in pitching checkout.

6
00:00:36.946 --> 00:00:38.869
PMM: And was it the portal?

7
00:00:39.669 --> 00:00:45.900
Dana: Kind of? The portal was the excuse.

8
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:52.131
Dana: The real thing, and I didn't get this until maybe the third call, was they had a new CFO. Ines.

9
00:00:52.131 --> 00:00:58.362
Dana: She'd been there about four months, and her first month-end close took nine days.

10
00:00:58.362 --> 00:01:04.592
Dana: A chunk of that was two people in finance matching deposits from the bank against the processor statements against invoices in the ERP.

11
00:01:04.592 --> 00:01:10.823
Dana: By hand, in spreadsheets. She said, and I wrote this down, "month-end is a week of archaeology."

12
00:01:11.623 --> 00:01:15.469
PMM: Who made the final call, and how do you know?

13
00:01:16.269 --> 00:01:21.397
Dana: Ines. A hundred percent.

14
00:01:21.397 --> 00:01:26.526
Dana: I know because the IT director, Paul, who was my day-to-day, told me straight up, "I can recommend, she decides." And she's the one who signed.

15
00:01:26.526 --> 00:01:31.654
Dana: She's also the one who sat on the first proposal.

16
00:01:32.454 --> 00:01:33.954
PMM: Sat on it?

17
00:01:34.754 --> 00:01:40.267
Dana: We sent the first proposal to Paul and it was all about the portal. The checkout, the APIs.

18
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:45.779
Dana: Then nothing happened for three weeks.

19
00:01:45.779 --> 00:01:51.292
Dana: Turned out Paul had forwarded it to her, she read the first page and said it wasn't her problem.

20
00:01:52.092 --> 00:01:55.554
PMM: What did the customer bring up about the competitor?

21
00:01:56.354 --> 00:02:09.046
Dana: Fiserv was the incumbent, through their bank. I honestly don't know what Fiserv showed them, if anything. I never saw a deck.

22
00:02:09.046 --> 00:02:21.738
Dana: What Ines told me was that when she called the bank to say they were looking around, the answer she got was, "we can take a look at your rates." And she said, "I didn't ask about my rates." That's about all I've got.

23
00:02:22.538 --> 00:02:26.769
PMM: What changed in their questions after they'd talked to the bank?

24
00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:35.454
Dana: Not much. If anything she got more annoyed. She did ask us one new thing afterwards, whether she'd get a named contact.

25
00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:43.338
Dana: Her bank rep had changed and she didn't know who the new one was. I said yes, me. [laughs]

26
00:02:44.138 --> 00:02:48.754
PMM: Which part of our pitch got a reaction, and which got silence?

27
00:02:49.554 --> 00:02:56.413
Dana: Silence was the whole opening. I ran the standard first-call deck. Financial infrastructure, global growth, all the currencies.

28
00:02:56.413 --> 00:03:03.272
Dana: They sell to restaurants in three states. Paul was polite about it. Ines wasn't on that call, thank god.

29
00:03:03.272 --> 00:03:10.131
Dana: And the developer story went nowhere. Paul's team is four people and they aren't building anything.

30
00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:16.990
Dana: The portal is off the shelf with a plugin. The reaction was the payout reconciliation.

31
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:23.849
Dana: Our solutions engineer, Wen, showed how each payout ties back to the individual transactions and how that feeds the ERP.

32
00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:30.708
Dana: Ines actually stopped him and said, "go back." Then she asked if she could see it with their data.

33
00:03:31.508 --> 00:03:36.123
PMM: When did you first feel this was won? What happened right before?

34
00:03:36.923 --> 00:03:42.747
Dana: That meeting. Well, the one after.

35
00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:48.571
Dana: Wen asked for one month of their statements, the controller sent them over, and Wen and I sat with the controller and worked out how many hours the matching took.

36
00:03:48.571 --> 00:03:54.396
Dana: It was something like 60 hours a month across two people.

37
00:03:54.396 --> 00:04:00.220
Dana: Then Wen put a dollar figure on it using their own cost for those people, plus the write-offs from deposits they never managed to match.

38
00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:06.044
Dana: I don't remember the exact figure. Low six figures a year.

39
00:04:06.044 --> 00:04:11.868
Dana: Ines looked at it and said, "that's my number, not yours." Meaning it was built from her data.

40
00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:17.692
Dana: After that it was paperwork.

41
00:04:18.492 --> 00:04:21.954
PMM: Before that meeting, where did you think it stood?

42
00:04:22.754 --> 00:04:25.831
Dana: Dead, honestly. I had it at 20 percent.

43
00:04:26.631 --> 00:04:30.092
PMM: What did they ask for that we couldn't show?

44
00:04:30.892 --> 00:04:38.277
Dana: A reference. She wanted to talk to another distributor. Wholesale, invoices, that kind of business. We had retail stories and software stories.

45
00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:45.662
Dana: I sent her a retail case study and she said, "this is a company that sells sweaters." We never got her a call.

46
00:04:45.662 --> 00:04:53.046
Dana: She signed anyway, but she brought it up twice. Oh, and the branches.

47
00:04:53.046 --> 00:05:00.431
Dana: They have 14 branches with counter sales, and she asked if the card readers at the counter would land in the same report.

48
00:05:00.431 --> 00:05:07.815
Dana: Wen said yes and showed a slide. We didn't demo it. She let it go.

49
00:05:08.615 --> 00:05:12.077
PMM: What exact words did they use for their problem?

50
00:05:12.877 --> 00:05:20.377
Dana: "A week of archaeology," that was hers.

51
00:05:20.377 --> 00:05:27.877
Dana: The one she said most was, "I just want to know where my money is." She never once said "payments." She'd say "the deposits." Paul would say "the processor." Nobody said "infrastructure."

52
00:05:28.677 --> 00:05:30.215
PMM: Anything you'd do differently?

53
00:05:31.015 --> 00:05:35.246
Dana: Find the CFO sooner. I lost a month pitching a portal.
